cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Privacy Explorer: An Implementation-Centred Reference for PET Combinations, Sectoral Adoption, and Assurance Artefacts"
abstract: >
  Privacy Explorer is a companion repository to a survey of privacy-enhancing
  technology (PET) adoption in real-world applications. It encodes the
  survey's central argument — that PET viability is determined by whether
  privacy claims can be rendered into repeatable, inspectable artefacts —
  as a set of relational reference tables (PET primitives, two-PET pairings,
  three-PET stacks, sectoral deployment contexts), a conceptual index of
  common exposure problems, a glossary of formalised terms, and YAPS, a
  rule-based assurance-gap tool for assessing specific PET deployments.
  The repository is intended to support workshops, design exercises, and
  ongoing cumulative work rather than to constitute a prescriptive standard.
